  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Typical late-summer weather is expected, with \nmostly isolated late-day showers and storms for the next several \ndays.\n\nNear-normal deep-layer moisture, proximity to deep sub-tropical \nridge in the western Atlantic, and minimal mesoscale forcing \nmechanisms, should keep coverage of showers/storms to isolated to \nlocally scattered over the next several days. Coverage of \nshowers/storms will be locally greatest each day focused around weak \nsurface pressure trough over the Piedmont, the sea breeze as well as \nwhere moderate low-lvl instability develops, which varies by day.\n\nMost showers/storms that are able to develop through the afternoon \nand evening will remain relatively shallow and consist of mostly \nshowers with isolated/embedded storms with infrequent lightning. \nDowndrafts will be equally typical for summer-time storms and \nproduce 20-30 mph wind gusts immediately surrounding the locations \nwhere they collapse.\n\nIsolated and infrequent strong to locally severe storms can't be \nruled out given the moderate to strong instability in place within \nthe hot/humid air mass over the Carolinas (MLCAPE of 2000-3500 \nJ/kg). Clustering storms that collapse along a common cold pool will \nhave the best chance to produce 40-60 mph wind gusts given the lack \nof deep-layer shear to otherwise organize storms (0-6 km shear < 20 \nkts). Based on low-lvl shear vector, continued storm development \nwill be most likely along the northeast to easterly quadrant of any \noutflow boundary each day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Risk for hot temperatures and dangerous heat \nincrease early next week.\n\nStrong sub-tropical ridging over the western Atlantic will continue \nto be suppressed south through this weekend before retrograding and \nelongating westward across FL and into the northeast Gulf into early \nnext week. Central NC is expected to situated between two bands of \nseasonably anomalous deep-layer moisture Sun into Wed, which should \nlimit widespread convective chances, although isolated \nshowers/storms are still possible. Increasing convective debris and \nlingering small-scale forcing may couple with a band of deeper \nmoisture from the Midwest and northern Mid-Atlantic as it sags south \nand into the Carolinas Wed night onward which will complicate the \nrisk for continued dangerous heat beyond Wed. \n\nIn the absence of convection, building sub-tropical ridge to our \nsouth and warming H850 temperatures to around 20C would support low-\nmid 90s as early as Sun, and as high as upper 90s possible Tues \nthrough Fri. Moisture and associated heat index values during peak \nheating will largely depend on the position/location of a persist \nPiedmont trough, with drier downsloping flow to its west and more \nhumid conditions to its east. Locations east of this feature, and \nthat remain void of convection, will likely reach Moderate HeatRisk \nand eclipse 105 degree heat indices during peak heating.",
